As a first application we have constructed a NN PES for ZnO using our NN program RuNNer. \({ }^{25}\) The reference DFT calculations have been carried out with the FHI-aims package \({ }^{26}\) employing a basis set of numerical atomic orbitals and the PBE functional. \({ }^{27}\) All settings have been carefully tested to ensure a convergence of energy differences to less than 1 meV per atom for all structures.

To construct the NN potential, we have calculated 7366 \(\mathrm{Zn}_{N} \mathrm{O}_{N}\) clusters ( \(N=1-40\) ), 26037 ZnO bulk structures including crystal structures, random structures, and snapshots from MD simulations, and 5347 slab geometries. This data set has been split into a training set ( \(90 \%\) ) to fit the parameters of the NNs and an independent test set \((10 \%)\) to check the predictive power. In order to fit the short-range energies, the short-range energy contributions for each structure have been extracted from the DFT total energies by first subtracting the electrostatic energies arising from the DFT Hirshfeld charges. The resulting short-range energies as well as their gradients have been used to fit the parameters of the short-range NNs (two hidden layers with 20 nodes per layer) using standard gradient-based iterative fitting algorithms. The root mean squared errors (RMSE) of the short-range energies is about \(0.0020 \mathrm{eV} /\) atom . For the test set the RMSEs is \(0.0029 \mathrm{eV} /\) atom . For fitting the atomic charges we have used NNs containing two hidden layers with 40 nodes each. The resulting RMSE of the charges is \(0.0088 e\) /atom (test set: \(0.0090 e /\) atom). The RMSEs of the total energies and total forces from the combined short-range and electrostatic contributions are

