The accuracy of the atomic forces is essential for performing MD simulations. In Fig. 4 the NN forces of a \(\mathrm{Zn}_{15} \mathrm{O}_{15}\) cluster are compared with the DFT values. A similar agreement has been found also for bulk systems. The precise representation of the atomic charges is illustrated in Fig. 5 showing a comparison of the NN and DFT Hirshfeld charges of the zinc and oxygen atoms in the same cluster.

The results obtained for ZnO show that NN potentials are able to provide very accurate PESs for multicomponent systems. However, due to the intrinsically nonphysical, i.e., unbiased, functional form of NNs, a large set of reference data is needed to ensure that the NN potential has "learned" the relevant physics of the system. Therefore, the computational costs to set up a NN potential are higher than for other types of potentials. Nevertheless, this effort pays off quickly, because MD simulations employing the NN potential are about \(4-5\) orders of magnitude faster than on-the-fly \(a b\) initio MD simulations. A drawback of NN potentials is the limited transferability to structures that are very different from the structures included in the training set. The potential presented above thus cannot be expected to describe pure metallic zinc or molecular oxygen in its current parametrization, and as for any empirical potential, there is no guarantee that the potential will be correct in all situations. Nevertheless, a major advantage of the NN approach is that whenever electronic structure data become available for such structures, they can easily be incorporated into the training set of the NN to refine the potential and to extend its range of validity without any change in the functional form. In practical applications, a limitation is posed by the exponentially growing configuration space if a large number of chemical species is present. We believe that NN potentials of the presented form will be most

