In summary, we have presented a neural network scheme to evaluate the energy and forces for multicomponent systems, which can be applied to large numbers of atoms. We have overcome the limitation of the NN method to systems with a single atomic species by introducing long-range electrostatic interactions based on environment-dependent charges. Thus, the method is now applicable to systems of arbitrary chemical composition. It is several orders of magnitude faster than electronic structure calculations, but the accuracy is essentially
the same. No physical approximations have been introduced apart from the chosen reference electronic structure method. The method is general and works for clusters, surfaces, and bulk structures, which can be crystalline or amorphous. We expect the method to be most useful for molecular dynamics simulations of large systems and extensive sampling problems, which are computationally too expensive to be carried out directly employing ab initio methods.

